    <body>
      <sec id="article-30093.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Thrombocytopenia is a hematologic condition defined by platelet counts below 150,000/&#x000b5;L. Platelets play a vital role in hemostasis and wound repair, and low levels can increase risks of bleeding and, paradoxically, thrombosis. This course reviews the clinical presentation of thrombocytopenia which varies with severity, from asymptomatic cases in mild disease to life-threatening hemorrhage in severe forms. Participants will also gain understanding of careful history-taking, physical examination, and laboratory evaluation crucial to uncover underlying etiologies of&#x000a0;thrombocytopenia, eg, autoimmune disorders, infections, malignancies, or drug reactions and the need for prompt recognition of emergent conditions, including heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, hemolytic uremic syndrome, and pregnancy-associated complications, essential for reducing morbidity and mortality.</p>
        <p>This course explores the pathophysiology, evaluation, and management of thrombocytopenia as well as distinguishing features between mild, moderate, and severe cases, red flag findings, and evidence-based treatment strategies tailored to etiology and risk level. Diagnostic tools and evolving therapies, including second-generation thrombopoietin receptor agonists are also reviewed.&#x000a0;This activity for healthcare professionals is designed to enhance the learner's competence in accurately identifying thrombocytopenia, performing the recommended evaluation, applying therapeutic decision-making, and implementing an appropriate interprofessional approach when managing this condition&#x000a0;to enhance patient safety in both acute and chronic care settings and to optimize outcomes for patients across the spectrum of thrombocytopenia.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the clinical features associated with the various severities of thrombocytopenia.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Apply evidence-based diagnostic strategies, including history-taking, physical examination, and laboratory testing, to evaluate&#x000a0;thrombocytopenia.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select appropriate treatment approaches for a patient with thrombocytopenia.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with the&#x000a0;interprofessional team to provide holistic care when treating patients with thrombocytopenia.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=30093&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=30093">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Thrombocytopenia is characterized by a platelet count that falls below the established normal threshold, specifically 150,000/&#x000b5;L in adults. Platelets are essential blood components involved in the process of hemostasis and wound repair. The associated risks of thrombocytopenia vary from negligible to heightened risks of hemorrhage and thrombosis. The relationship between thrombocytopenia severity and bleeding risk remains inconclusive.</p>
        <p>Mild thrombocytopenia (100,000-150,000/&#x000b5;L) is generally asymptomatic. Moderate thrombocytopenia (50,000-100,000/&#x000b5;L) may manifest symptoms, eg, easy bruising.&#x000a0;Severe thrombocytopenia (below 50,000/&#x000b5;L) can lead to bleeding during surgical procedures, and individuals with platelet counts under 10,000/&#x000b5;L are more susceptible to spontaneous hemorrhage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r1">[1]</xref>&#x000a0;Additionally, thrombocytopenia may coexist with thrombotic events in conditions including heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT), antiphospholipid antibody syndrome (APS), disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), thrombotic microangiopathy, and paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria (PNH).</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Common Causes of Thrombocytopenia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Primary immune thrombocytopenia (ITP) represents an autoimmune disorder in which antibodies target platelets, leading to their destruction. Secondary forms of ITP can occur in association with other conditions or triggers. Drug-induced ITP includes reactions to medications, eg, quinine, sulfonamides, ampicillin, vancomycin, piperacillin, acetaminophen, ibuprofen, naproxen, cimetidine, and glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors. Certain foods, including African beans, sesame seeds, walnuts, and cranberry juice, may contribute, and some herbal supplements, eg, Taxus yunnanensis (Chinese yew), Rumex crispus (yellow dock), Arctium lappa (burdock), green tea, guarana, and ginseng, have been implicated. HIT involves antiplatelet antibodies that activate platelets, resulting in arterial and venous thrombosis.</p>
        <p>Autoimmune disorders, including systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), rheumatoid arthritis, and immune-mediated thyroid disease, can cause thrombocytopenia, as can lymphoproliferative diseases (eg, chronic lymphocytic leukemia). Infectious causes include viral infections&#x02014;HIV, hepatitis C, Epstein-Barr virus, parvovirus, mumps, varicella, rubella, and Zika virus&#x02014;sepsis-related bone marrow suppression, <italic toggle="yes">Helicobacter pylori</italic> infection, tick-borne infections like <italic toggle="yes">Leptospirosis</italic>,<italic toggle="yes">&#x000a0;Brucellosis</italic>, <italic toggle="yes">Anaplasmosis</italic>, and intracellular parasites, eg, <italic toggle="yes">Plasmodium</italic> and <italic toggle="yes">Babesia</italic> species. Hypersplenism secondary to chronic liver disease also contributes.</p>
        <p>Drug-induced nonimmune thrombocytopenia results from dose-dependent suppression of platelet production, observed with valproic acid, daptomycin, linezolid, and chronic alcohol use. Nutritional deficiencies in folate, vitamin B12, or copper can impair platelet production. Pregnancy-related thrombocytopenia varies from mild in gestational thrombocytopenia to moderate or severe in preeclampsia and HELLP (hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, low platelet count) syndrome.&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r1">[1]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r2">[2]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r5">[5]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r6">[6]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r8">[8]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r9">[9]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r10">[10]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r11">[11]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r13">[13]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Additional&#x000a0;<bold>Thrombocytopenia Etiologies</bold></bold>
</p>
        <p>Other causes include malignancies with bone marrow suppression, eg, leukemias, lymphomas, solid tumors, and myelodysplasia, as well as malignancies associated with chronic or subclinical disseminated intravascular coagulation. Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria, thrombotic microangiopathies including thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) and hemolytic uremic syndrome (HUS), antiphospholipid antibody syndrome, and aplastic anemia represent additional etiologies. Inherited thrombocytopenias, often presenting in children, include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Von Willebrand disease type 2B</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Alport syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Wiskott-Aldrich syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Fanconi syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Thrombocytopenia-absent radius syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Bernard&#x02013;Soulier syndrome</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>May-Hegglin anomaly&#x000a0;<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r14">[14]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r15">[15]</xref></p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>The typical range for platelet count varies according to age, sex, and ethnicity. Female, younger, and Black individuals tend to have marginally higher platelet levels.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r16">[16]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Decreased Platelet Production</bold>
</p>
        <p>Thrombocytopenia can result from decreased platelet production due to bone marrow failure, as seen in aplastic anemia and paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria. Certain drugs, including valproic acid, daptomycin, select chemotherapy agents, and radiation therapy, can suppress bone marrow function. Chronic alcohol use and inherited thrombocytopenias also impair platelet production. Viral infections and systemic conditions, eg, nutritional deficiencies in folate or vitamin B12, sepsis, and myelodysplastic syndromes further compromise marrow function. In these scenarios, the production of platelets, red blood cells, and white blood cells is impaired, resulting in thrombocytopenia, anemia, and leukopenia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r17">[17]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Increased Platelet Destruction</bold>
</p>
        <p>Under normal conditions, monocytes and macrophages within the reticuloendothelial system remove aging platelets, which typically have a lifespan of 8 to 10 days. In ITP, antiplatelet autoantibodies attach to platelets and megakaryocytes, enhancing platelet destruction and reducing production. These antibodies can arise in primary ITP, drug-induced ITP, lymphoproliferative disorders, autoimmune diseases (eg, SLE), and chronic infections, including hepatitis C, HIV, and Helicobacter pylori. Nonimmune platelet destruction occurs in patients with mechanical heart valves, preeclampsia or HELLP syndrome, DIC, and thrombotic microangiopathy, where platelet consumption and thrombus formation drive thrombocytopenia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r19">[19]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r20">[20]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r21">[21]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r23">[23]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Dilutional Thrombocytopenia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Extensive fluid resuscitation or blood transfusion can lead to dilutional thrombocytopenia. Increased plasma volume during pregnancy contributes to dilutional effects, representing one of several mechanisms underlying gestational thrombocytopenia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r24">[24]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r25">[25]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Redistribution of Platelets</bold>
</p>
        <p>Approximately one-third of platelets reside in the spleen. Conditions that cause splenomegaly, eg, splenic congestion from cirrhosis, can sequester platelets, thereby reducing the number of circulating platelets and contributing to thrombocytopenia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r26">[26]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>
<bold>Review of Peripheral Blood Smear</bold>
</p>
        <p>Examining platelet size and morphology on a peripheral blood smear assists in identifying conditions associated with increased platelet destruction or consumption. Platelet clumping can occur when EDTA is used as an anticoagulant, resulting in pseudothrombocytopenia.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r27">[27]</xref> Platelet adhesion to polymorphonuclear cells, a phenomenon known as &#x0201c;platelet satellitism,&#x0201d; can also be detected through careful microscopic examination of the smear.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r28">[28]</xref>&#x000a0;Giant platelets often indicate inherited disorders, eg, Alport syndrome and Bernard&#x02013;Soulier syndrome (see<bold>&#x000a0;Image</bold>. Bernard Soulier Syndrome).</p>
        <p>Assessment of white blood cell (WBC) and red blood cell (RBC) morphology can provide additional diagnostic clues indicative of a specific condition. Schistocytes, or fragmented RBCs, are characteristic of thrombotic microangiopathic conditions and reflect intravascular hemolysis (see<bold>&#x000a0;Image</bold>.&#x000a0;Intravascular Hemolysis). Teardrop cells, nucleated RBCs, and leukoerythroblastic features suggest bone marrow infiltration, while the presence of immature WBCs may indicate leukemia. Nutritional deficiencies causing megaloblastic anemia manifest as hypersegmented neutrophils and macro-ovalocytes, which are large oval-shaped RBCs with reduced or absent central pallor, providing critical information about underlying hematologic disorders (see<bold>&#x000a0;Image</bold>. Macrocytic Anemia).</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>
<bold>Clinical History</bold>
</p>
        <p>A comprehensive history plays a central role in determining the etiology of thrombocytopenia. Patients with platelet counts greater than 50,000/&#x000b5;L usually remain asymptomatic, whereas those with platelet counts below 20,000/&#x000b5;L often present with spontaneous bleeding. Clarifying prior blood count results and establishing a baseline platelet level are essential, since a recent decline raises concern.</p>
        <p>A detailed bleeding history should include petechiae, hemorrhagic events, gingival bleeding, and epistaxis. Oral purpura and gross hematuria warrant special attention, as they may precede severe hemorrhage associated with ITP (see<bold>&#x000a0;Image</bold>. Petechial Rash).<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r29">[29]</xref> The clinician should also explore potential infectious exposures, including viral, bacterial, and tick-borne illnesses, while assessing HIV risk factors. Travel to malaria-, dengue-, or Ebola-endemic regions further increases the index of suspicion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r30">[30]</xref> A diet history helps identify nutritional deficiencies. Additional important conditions include systemic lupus erythematosus, rheumatoid arthritis, prior bariatric surgery, and a history of blood transfusion.</p>
        <p>Medication review must cover prescription drugs, over-the-counter products, quinine-containing beverages, herbal teas, and supplements. Hospitalized patients require evaluation for exposure to heparin products. A family history should be assessed for thrombocytopenia or other bleeding disorders. In pregnant patients, symptoms, eg, headache, vision changes, abdominal pain, or flu-like illness, may point to preeclampsia or HELLP syndrome.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Physical Examination</bold>
</p>
        <p>A thorough physical examination involves inspecting the skin and mucosa for bleeding manifestations and assessing the liver, spleen, and lymph nodes. Thrombocytopenia-related bleeding typically presents as petechiae, non-palpable purpura, and ecchymosis. Dry purpura affects the skin, while wet purpura involves mucous membranes. Hepatomegaly and splenomegaly suggest conditions such as lymphoma, chronic liver disease, or other hematologic disorders. Lymphadenopathy may indicate infection, autoimmune disease, lymphoma, or malignancy.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>
<bold>Assessment of Isolated Thrombocytopenia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Evaluation of patients with isolated thrombocytopenia begins with a complete blood count (CBC), peripheral blood smear, and testing for HIV and hepatitis C virus. A repeat CBC helps confirm thrombocytopenia and rule out laboratory error.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Assessment of Thrombocytopenia-Associated Conditions</bold>
</p>
        <p>Additional findings on laboratory studies or clinical features may indicate the need for further testing to assess for underlying etiologic conditions. The presence of anemia with thrombocytopenia often points to infections, DIC, sepsis, thrombotic microangiopathy, or autoimmune disorders, eg, Felty syndrome, a complication of rheumatoid arthritis. Leukocytosis with thrombocytopenia may indicate an infection, malignancy, or chronic inflammatory disease, while pancytopenia suggests a myelodysplastic syndrome.&#x000a0;Patients with features of autoimmune disorders, including SLE and APS, require testing for antinuclear and antiphospholipid antibodies. In patients with both thrombocytopenia and thrombosis, evaluation should include platelet factor 4 antibodies for HIT, antiphospholipid antibodies for APS, and coagulation studies, eg, PT, aPTT, fibrinogen, and LDH, to assess for DIC and paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria.</p>
        <p>Liver enzymes and coagulation profiles should be checked in patients with liver disease. A bone marrow biopsy becomes necessary when the etiology of thrombocytopenia remains unclear or a hematologic disorder is suspected. Bone marrow findings provide important diagnostic information. Normal-to-high megakaryocyte counts indicate increased platelet destruction, while reduced megakaryocytes with decreased other cell lines point toward aplastic anemia. Severe reduction or absence of megakaryocytes in SLE results from autoantibodies directed against the thrombopoietin receptor. Additional findings include megaloblastic changes in red blood cells (RBCs) and granulocytes in vitamin B12, folate, or copper deficiency, while dysplastic features of hematopoietic cells are seen in myelodysplastic syndromes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>
<bold>Thrombocytopenia Management Approach</bold>
</p>
        <p>In patients with asymptomatic, mild&#x000a0;thrombocytopenia, repeat CBC and routine monitoring is the recommended management. However, thrombocytopenia&#x000a0;emergencies that require immediate action include HIT, TTP, HUS, drug-induced ITP, pregnancy with severe thrombocytopenia, bleeding with severe thrombocytopenia, urgently needed invasive procedures in the presence of severe thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, and aplastic anemia. In these cases, management relies on identifying the underlying cause and addressing it. For instance, TTP would be best treated with plasma exchange.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r31">[31]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r29">[29]</xref>&#x000a0;Additionally, in patients with bleeding and severe thrombocytopenia, treatment includes platelet transfusion.&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>
<bold>Primary Immune Thrombocytopenia</bold>
</p>
        <p>This condition is a diagnosis of exclusion. First-line treatment includes glucocorticoids and intravenous immune globulins (IVIG); these agents inhibit autoantibody production and platelet degradation. Second-line treatment includes rituximab, immunosuppressive drugs, and splenectomy. Third-line agents are thrombopoietin receptor agonists such as romiplostim or eltrombopag, which stimulate platelet production.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r30">[30]</xref>&#x000a0;</p>
        <p>Since the COVID-19 pandemic, the treatment of thrombocytopenia has shifted from immunosuppressants and splenectomy to second-generation thrombopoietin receptor agonists. These agents, structurally distinct from endogenous thrombopoietin (unlike first-generation thrombopoietin receptor agonists), avoid cross-reactivity and worsening thrombocytopenia.&#x000a0;In about one-third of patients, they&#x000a0;provide longer treatment-free periods&#x000a0;with stable platelet counts compared to immunosuppressants.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r32">[32]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Drug-Induced Thrombocytopenia</bold>
</p>
        <p>Discontinuation of the offending drug typically leads to recovery of platelet counts in cases of drug-induced thrombocytopenia. The cornerstone of therapy for HIT involves immediate withdrawal of all heparin products, followed by initiation of anticoagulant agents that inhibit antithrombin and anti-Xa activity. Once the platelet count returns to normal levels, dicoumarol agents may be introduced to maintain anticoagulation.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r33">[33]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r34">[34]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r29">[29]</xref></p>
        <p>Management of secondary ITP focuses on treating the underlying condition. In patients with systemic lupus erythematosus, immunosuppressive agents help control disease activity and improve platelet counts. For those with <italic toggle="yes">Helicobacter pylori</italic>&#x02013;associated thrombocytopenia, eradication therapy not only addresses the infection but also promotes hematologic recovery.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r35">[35]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r36">[36]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Perioperative T<bold>hrombocytopenia</bold></bold>
</p>
        <p>Prophylactic platelet transfusions for preoperative thrombocytopenia are not well-supported by evidence and may potentially result in refractory thrombocytopenia. Alternatively, immunosuppressants, thrombopoietin receptor agonists, and monoclonal antibodies have demonstrated effectiveness in normalizing platelet counts in some patients. Intraoperatively, desmopressin and antifibrinolytic agents may be used prophylactically, while recombinant factor VII can be used to treat severe hemorrhage.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r37">[37]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Pseudothrombocytopenia may arise due to&#x000a0;<italic toggle="yes">in vitro</italic>&#x000a0;platelet aggregation caused by ethylenediaminetetraacetic acid (EDTA)-dependent agglutinins, improperly anticoagulated specimens, and glycoprotein IIb/IIIa inhibitors. Additionally, large platelets might be misidentified as white blood cells by automated counters. Additionally, platelet dysfunction can be misdiagnosed as thrombocytopenia. Qualitative platelet defects have been noted in renal disease, eg, uremia, and liver disease.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r38">[38]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r39">[39]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>In the context of thrombocytopenia, the prognosis depends on&#x000a0;the underlying cause of the patient's condition. Compared to primary ITP, secondary ITP is associated with a poorer survival rate; the median survival period is reduced by 5.1 years and 11.1 years, respectively. In the majority of cases, the causes of mortality included severe hemorrhaging, hematologic malignancies, and infection.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r40">[40]</xref>&#x000a0;In a prospective study, patients with mild&#x000a0;thrombocytopenia (100,000 to 150,000/&#x000b5;L) were found to have a 10-year probability of developing autoimmune disorders of 12%.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r41">[41]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Severe thrombocytopenia can result in life-threatening internal bleeding, and involvement of the brain may lead to fatal outcomes. The complications largely depend on the underlying cause of thrombocytopenia. HIT may progress to deep venous thrombosis, pulmonary embolism, cerebrovascular accident, or myocardial infarction.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r42">[42]</xref> APS often manifests with both arterial and venous thrombosis and may also contribute to spontaneous abortions.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r43">[43]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r44">[44]</xref>&#x000a0;TTP causes widespread microvascular thrombosis affecting multiple organs, with the central nervous system most commonly involved. HUS predominantly damages the kidneys, although both TTP and HUS can advance to multiorgan failure when untreated.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s13" sec-type="Consultations">
        <title>Consultations</title>
        <p>A newly diagnosed case of thrombocytopenia should prompt referral to a hematologist for confirmation and further evaluation. In instances of unexplained thrombocytopenia, consulting a hematologist is also highly advisable. Immediate consultation is essential for life-threatening conditions, including TTP, HUS, HIT, aplastic anemia, leukemia, and other conditions leading to bone marrow failure.&#x000a0;Pregnant patients with HELLP/preeclampsia prompt an obstetrician or maternal-fetal medicine consultation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s14" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Preventing complications of severe thrombocytopenia requires early recognition of the underlying cause and proactive patient education. Patients must understand the importance of timely medical evaluation when new bleeding symptoms occur, including petechiae, unexplained bruising, or mucosal bleeding. Education should emphasize avoidance of medications, supplements, or herbal products known to exacerbate thrombocytopenia, as well as the risks linked to alcohol use. In particular, clinicians should inform patients to avoid over-the-counter agents that impair platelet function, including aspirin, NSAIDs, and ginkgo biloba. Additionally, warn against quinine-containing beverages and certain herbal teas, which may contribute to thrombocytopenia. Clinicians should counsel patients with conditions, eg, HIT, APS, TTP, or HUS, about the potential severity of complications and the need for strict adherence to treatment and follow-up.</p>
        <p>Patient education should also include strategies to minimize the risk of bleeding in daily life. Guidance may include avoiding contact sports, using a soft-bristled toothbrush, and implementing injury-prevention measures at home. Patients must be educated on activity restrictions based on platelet count. Those with counts &#x0003e;50,000/&#x000b5;L should exercise caution with contact sports, while these activities should be completely avoided if platelet counts are &#x0003c;10,000/&#x000b5;L.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r41">[41]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-30093.r45">[45]</xref>&#x000a0;Women of childbearing age should be counseled on pregnancy-related risks, particularly in APS. Clear communication about signs of thrombotic events, such as chest pain, neurologic changes, or leg swelling, empowers patients to seek emergency care promptly, ultimately improving safety and outcomes.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-30093.s15" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Thrombocytopenia, defined by a platelet count below 150,000/&#x000b5;L, encompasses a spectrum of clinical presentations ranging from asymptomatic mild cases to severe forms with life-threatening bleeding or thrombotic complications. Platelets are essential for hemostasis, and their deficiency can occur from autoimmune disorders, infections, drug reactions, hematologic malignancies, or perioperative factors. Accurate assessment relies on a thorough history, physical examination, and targeted laboratory evaluation, including CBC, peripheral smear, and serologic or bone marrow studies. Prompt recognition of emergencies such as heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura, hemolytic uremic syndrome, or pregnancy-associated thrombocytopenia is critical to reducing morbidity and mortality.</p>
        <p>Effective management requires coordinated, interprofessional care. Physicians, advanced practitioners, and general practitioners apply evidence-based diagnostic algorithms and initiate appropriate therapies, including immunosuppressants, intravenous immune globulins, or thrombopoietin receptor agonists. Nurses and pharmacists monitor patient responses, medication interactions, and laboratory trends, providing education and ensuring adherence to safety protocols. Collaboration and communication among the care team optimize patient-centered care, facilitate early intervention for high-risk cases, and enhance overall outcomes and team performance in managing thrombocytopenia.</p>
      </sec>
